  "id": "Compound::DB00599 - Gene::2557",
  "verbalization": "Thiopental, a barbiturate, is shown to bind to Gabra6, a subunit of the GABA-A receptor, and this binding is linked to the participation of Gabra6 in GABA-A receptor activation and in benzodiazepine receptor activity. These pathways ultimately lead to the participation of Gabra4 in these processes. The evidence suggests a potential mechanism by which Thiopental may influence Gabra4, though the exact nature of this interaction remains unclear. The scores from the paths indicate a consistent but moderate level of support for the relationship, likely due to the shared involvement of Gabra6 in multiple receptor activation pathways. The presence of multiple pathways involving Gabra6 and its subunits implies that Thiopental may modulate GABAergic signaling through a complex network of receptor interactions. The integration of these pathways highlights the importance of Gabra6 in mediating the effects of Thiopental, though further experimental validation is needed to confirm the direct role of Gabra4 in this process.",
